Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns, their potential usage & likely conditions  
• "application_kind": identify utility patents (commonly value = ‘U’; occasionally ‘A’ is used for U‑S utility applications).  
• "country_code": if you only want U‑S utility patents you can filter on ‘US’.  
• "grant_date": to keep patents whose grant year = 2010  →  WHERE "grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20101231.  
• "filing_date": needed for the 10‑year forward‑citation window (compare citing patent filing date with the focal patent filing date).  
• "publication_number": unique identifier of the (granted) patent that will be “cited”.  
• "application_number": unique identifier of the citing application.  
• "citation": VARIANT column that contains the list of backward references made by each publication.  
  – inside each element of "citation" the key "publication_number" (and/or "application_number") gives the cited patent.  
  – flattening "citation" produces one row per cited document for the citing patent.  

Typical conditions you may need  
•  "application_kind" = 'U'                           -- utility patents  
•  "grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20101231         -- granted in 2010  
•  Date difference rule for the 10‑year window:  
   ABS(f.citing_filing – p."filing_date")  <= 100000  -- crude 10‑year test (YYMMDD → +10 0000)  
•  HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT f."application_number") = 1  -- exactly one forward citation

Ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries  
(Every column name is enclosed in double quotes; LIMIT 20 added for safety.)

```sql
-- 1. Preview a handful of grants in 2010
SELECT *
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20101231
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. What distinct values exist in "application_kind"?
SELECT DISTINCT "application_kind"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Take a quick look at U‑S utility patents granted in 2010
SELECT "publication_number",
       "application_number",
       "filing_date",
       "grant_date",
       "application_kind",
       "country_code"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20101231
  AND "country_code" = 'US'
  AND "application_kind" = 'U'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Inspect the raw citation array for one random patent
SELECT t."publication_number",
       f.value
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."citation") f
WHERE t."grant_date" = 20100112    -- choose any known 2010 grant date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Parse the citation objects to show the cited publication numbers
SELECT t."publication_number"               AS "citing_pub",
       f.value:"publication_number"::STRING AS "cited_pub"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."citation") f
WHERE t."grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20101231
  AND t."application_kind" = 'U'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
